<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Hattum</h1><p class="st-meta">2000 BCE – 1730 BCE (approx.) · capital Hattus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hattum, the Hattian city-state on the site of later Hattusa, cursed and destroyed by Anitta before it rose again as the Hittite capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hattus was a native Hattian town on a rocky ridge near modern Boğazkale in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. By the early 2nd millennium BCE it was the seat of a local king and hosted a small trading quarter of merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, part of the network centred on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kanesh">Kanesh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government beyond Old Assyrian letters and the later Hittite tradition. Its people spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattic-language">Hattic</a>, a language unrelated to Hittite or Semitic tongues, and their gods, such as the sun goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a>, were later absorbed into Hittite religion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1730 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta of Kussara</a> took the city after its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyusti">Piyusti</a> was defeated, razed it and sowed it with weeds, cursing any king who resettled it. A century later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a> did exactly that, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 2000 BCE – 1730 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:19.90%;width:5.37%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2000 BCE – 1730 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hattus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Piyusti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hattian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hattic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hattus, Hattian Hattusa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1865&amp;focus=hattum" aria-label="Hattum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5548 773 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hattum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5868 1029h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5868 1029h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=-1865&amp;focus=hattum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

